    "assumptions": [
      {
        "id": "A1",
        "text": "The utility is lost at t = 0 and stays lost for the 300 s horizon."
      },
      {
        "id": "A2",
        "text": "Only G1 receives a start command. G2 is not commanded in this scenario even when enabled; its settings do not affect the result."
      },
      {
        "id": "A3",
        "text": "Outage detection takes 0 ms: the G1 start command is issued at the outage instant."
      },
      {
        "id": "A4",
        "text": "G1 startup_delay_s is the time from the start command to G1 being ready to accept load."
      },
      {
        "id": "A5",
        "text": "Generator readiness and load transfer are separate events. Transfer takes 0 ms after readiness (simplification: instantaneous transfer)."
      },
      {
        "id": "A6",
        "text": "The battery inverter supplies the critical load from the outage instant with no interruption (simplification: no ride-through gap)."
      },
      {
        "id": "A7",
        "text": "After transfer G1 supplies up to its rated power; the battery supplies any residual load up to its inverter rating and remaining energy."
      },
      {
        "id": "A8",
        "text": "Available battery energy = usable_kwh x (initial_soc_pct - min_soc_pct) / 100, with no conversion losses and no recharging."
      },
      {
        "id": "A9",
        "text": "The critical load is constant at critical_kw. Unserved energy is demand supplied by neither G1 nor the battery."
      },
      {
        "id": "A10",
        "text": "Time and energy are tracked as exact integers (ms, kW*ms). Displayed kWh (6 decimals) and SoC (4 decimals) are rounded; requirements use the exact values."
      },
      {
        "id": "A11",
        "text": "Events with equal timestamps are ordered: utility_outage, start_generator, battery_depleted, g1_ready, g1_transfer_complete, horizon_reached. Events at or before the 300 s horizon are recorded."
      },
      {
        "id": "A12",
        "text": "battery_depleted is recorded at the first whole millisecond by which all available battery energy has been supplied while power is still requested; it is ordered before generator events at that timestamp. Frames are discrete state snapshots taken right after each event, not energy-integration intervals; energy metrics come from exact integer accounting, not from frames."
      },
      {
        "id": "A13",
        "text": "Synthetic, simplified demonstration model. Not Cummins equipment data and not a prediction of real equipment performance."
      }
